From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <daniel@matv.io>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] swstats_cpu: centre + enlarge the AF sharpness window

LOW-CONTRAST AF ROBUSTNESS + WINDOW MIS-PLACEMENT (OURS; companion to
the CDAF sweep-quality work in patches 07-10, 14). The soft-ISP CDAF
scores focus from the green squared-gradient metric
`stats_.sharpness += 1000 * Sum((g - g0)^2) / sumG`, accumulated only
where BOTH `(5*x)/window_.width == 3` and `(5*y)/window_.height == 3`
(patch 08). That predicate is true for x,y in [0.6, 0.8) of the frame,
i.e. the FOURTH fifth on each axis -- a single 20%x20% = 4% patch sitting
in the lower-right quadrant, NOT the centre (the centred fifth is index 2,
[0.4, 0.6)). The in-code comments elsewhere call this "centre of picture",
but the arithmetic never was.

Two consequences, both seen in the field on a ~1-2 m indoor shot of flat
white surfaces (washing machine / white cabinets / tiles) that locked at
the wrong distance and came out uniformly soft:

  1. Off-centre metering: the 4% patch samples the lower-right of the
     frame, so a subject the user framed centrally (where they aim) may
     fall entirely outside the AF window while off-axis clutter drives
     the lock.

  2. Tiny sampling area starves the metric on low-texture scenes. Over a
     4% patch of near-flat white the squared-gradient sum is a handful of
     sensor-noise counts, and dividing by the LARGE green sum of a bright
     white field drives it to near zero with high relative variance -- so
     the argmax over the ~21 coarse sweep positions is noise-driven and
     the lens lands at an arbitrary distance (the observed failure).

Fix: sample the centred middle third on each axis -- true when
`(3*x)/window_.width == 1` AND `(3*y)/window_.height == 1`, i.e.
x,y in [1/3, 2/3). That re-centres the window onto where the photographer
aims and grows it from 4% to ~11% of the frame (~2.8x the linear extent,
~9x the pixels sampled), which lifts the metric's signal-to-noise by ~3x
(sqrt of the sample count) on marginally-textured scenes and catches
texture the old off-centre patch missed. It also picks up more of any
in-frame edge (tile grout, appliance seams, cabinet gaps) even when the
dead-centre is blank.

The metric stays exactly ratiometric: `sharpness` and its per-line
normaliser `sumG` both scale with the window, and the AF logic only ever
compares sharpness against its own running peak (patches 07-10, 14), so
no threshold or sweep constant needs retuning. Pure widening of an
existing predicate -- no new state, no allocation, no change to the
squared-gradient kernel or the normalisation.

HONEST LIMIT: this cannot rescue a genuinely textureless target. Contrast-
detection AF (CDAF) has zero gradient to hill-climb on a perfectly flat
field regardless of window size; a bigger centred window only helps when
SOME texture exists in frame (as in the kitchen scene). True flat-white
focus needs phase-detect / hybrid AF hardware this contrast-only sensor
does not have. Follow-ups that need on-device tuning (multi-zone weighted
window, low-contrast hold-position guard) are deliberately left out of
this minimal, unambiguous change.
---
 src/libcamera/software_isp/swstats_cpu.cpp | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/libcamera/software_isp/swstats_cpu.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/software_isp/swstats_cpu.cpp
@@ -186,7 +186,7 @@ static constexpr unsigned int kBlueYMul = 29; /* 0.114 * 256 */
 	yVal += g * kGreenYMul;            \
 	yVal += b * kBlueYMul;             \
 	stats_.yHistogram[yVal * SwIspStats::kYHistogramSize / (256 * 256 * (div))]++; \
-	if (1 && r0 != 0 && ((5*x) / window_.width == 3) && ((5*y) / window_.height == 3)) \
+	if (1 && r0 != 0 && ((3*x) / window_.width == 1) && ((3*y) / window_.height == 1)) \
 	  sharpness += (g-g0) * (g-g0); \
 	r0 = r1; g0 = g1; b0 = b1; \
 	r1 = r; g1 = g; b1 = b; \
